-- title:
--   Decomposition group acts on second Igusa projection of norm-free points
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $M$ a positive integer with $5\le M$ and $p\nmid M$, and $Pl$ is a valuation subring of an algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$; $\kappa :=$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField ↥Pl` is assumed algebraically closed of characteristic $p$.
--
--   **Base field and model data.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, $\zeta\in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and $K$ is an intermediate field of $L\subseteq\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with `hK` saying that $K$ is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of Laurent series generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with `hAp` placing $p$ in its maximal ideal and `hζA` asserting that $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$; $A$ acts on $K$ compatibly with $L$. The element $j\in K$ is nonzero and has Laurent expansion the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157). The scheme [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) and its structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A (↥K) j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ — the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$ — are the two-chart model; it is assumed proper over $A$. Further algebra maps $A\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, $L\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with a scalar tower are given, together with $\rho:A\to Pl$ and the compatibilities `hρ`, `hAlgκ` of $\rho$ with $A\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and with $A\to\kappa$, and `hPl` saying $p$ lies in the non-units of $Pl$.
--
--   **The two components of the special fibre.** $C_1,C_2$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$, geometrically integral $\kappa$-schemes, closed-immersed by $i_1,i_2$ into the base change of the two-chart model to $\kappa$; `hcover` says the two images cover that base change, `hred` that $C_1\times C_2$ (the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1`) is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` that its number of points is $n>0$. Sections $\varepsilon$ (of the model over $A$), $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ (of $c_1$, $c_2$) are given, with `hε₁` identifying $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ with the base change of $\varepsilon$, and `hεC₂` asserting that the reduced section does not meet the image of $i_2$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the two-chart model (a scheme with structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), `hrep` asserts representability of the sub-Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero, and `hsm`, `hsep` that $D$ is smooth and separated over $A$; `hreps`, `hPk` give the corresponding representability over $\kappa$ and the comparison of Poincaré bundles with the base change of the Poincaré bundle over $A$; $D_1,D_2$ with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` do the same for $C_1,C_2$; `hDL`, `hPL` do the same over $L$, with `hsmL`, `hgiL`, `hprL`, `hgcL` recording smoothness and geometric integrality of the base change to $L$ and properness and geometric connectedness of $D_L$ over $L$. The morphism $\nu_2$ from $D_\kappa$ to $D_2$ is constrained by `hν₂`: pulling the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ back along a point composed with $\nu_2$ gives the rigidification of the restriction to $C_2$ of the corresponding bundle on $D_\kappa$.
--
--   **Geometric fibre and $q$-expansion pinning.** $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) together with an isomorphism $e\eta$ onto the geometric fibre of the two-chart model (`heη` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$); `hMηpin` pins the finite chart: every element of the chart algebra induces on $M\eta$ the function whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of its expansion in $K$. `hgal` asserts that for $g\in\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing $L$ pointwise, the bijection `Mη.pointEquivPlace` between $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and places is equivariant for [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54). Likewise `hgauss₁` pins $C_1$ through the models $Mdl_1,Mdl_2$ over $\kappa$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) attached to an integral weight-one form $w$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with nonvanishing reduction: isomorphisms $e_1:Mdl_1.C\cong C_1$, $e_2:Mdl_2.C\cong C_2$ over $\kappa$ are given (`he₁`, `he₂`), and for chart elements $a$ and power series $x,y$ over $A$ with $a\,y=x$ in Laurent series over $L$ and $\bar y\neq0$, the function induced on $Mdl_1$ has expansion $\bar x/\bar y$. `hεgal` records the Galois equivariance of $\varepsilon$ for any automorphism of the two-chart model that matches an automorphism of the chart algebra acting coefficientwise by $s\in\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$; `hΓA` makes the given semiring action of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ compatible with $A\to L$.
--
--   **Jacobian points, Hecke and Galois operators.** `gpts` is a bijection of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})=$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) (the degree-zero divisor class group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`) with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$; $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of $D$ over $A$ and $\tau$ assigns to each $s\in\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a semilinear automorphism of $D$; `hφmul`, `hφpts`, `hgadd`, `hτ1`, `hτmul`, `hτφ`, `hτpts` say that $\varphi$ is additive on points and matches the Hecke module structure through `gpts`, that `gpts` is additive, that $\tau$ is a monoid action commuting with $\varphi$, and that `gpts` is Galois-equivariant through $\tau$. `hin`, `hcomm` are the Hecke/diamond input and commutation data at level $Mp$. The Abel–Jacobi data over $L$ and $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ consist of $ajL$, $kL$, $ajbar$, $\bar\varepsilon$ with `hajLε`, `hajL` (pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x\cdot ajL$ is $\mathcal{O}(x)$ tensored with the ideal module of the section), `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, and `hpts_aj`: for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$ and $s$ of $M\eta$ with $s$ the cusp, the class of $[x]-[s]$ is carried by `gpts` to $x$ followed by $ajbar$.
--
--   **Special fibre group datum.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $J0s$, $JI$, $JE$, a subgroup `torus` of $J0s$ and a surjection $\mathrm{proj}:J0s\to JI\times JE$ with kernel `torus`. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify these groups with the $\kappa$-points of $D_\kappa$, $D_1$, $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` make them additive on Poincaré pullbacks, `hPTS` makes `pts` additive for the relative group law, and `hproj` identifies $\mathrm{proj}$ with restriction along $i_1$ and along $\nu_2$. $O$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePOpsV3.html#L13) (Hecke, diamond, inertia and related operators on $J0s$, $JI$, $JE$) and `hO` has four clauses: $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,\ell$ corresponds to $\varphi(\mathtt{heckeGenOne}\,\ell)$ for primes $\ell$; $O.\mathrm{diamondP}\,b$ and $O.\mathrm{diamondN}\,d$ correspond to $\varphi(\mathtt{diamondGen}\,d)$ under the stated congruences on $d$ modulo $M$ and $p$; and $O.\mathrm{inertia}\,b$ corresponds to $\tau s$ whenever $s\zeta=\zeta^{b}$. `frobT`, `hfrobT` give a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field acting on Laurent coefficients by $x\mapsto x^{p}$.
--
--   **Raynaud-type description of the special fibre.** $\mathrm{nodesIg}$ is a finite set of pairs of places of the Igusa function field, $\Psi$ an isomorphism of $J0s$ with [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0 κ F nodesIg`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L201) (classes of admissible triples: two degree-zero divisors vanishing at the nodes together with units at the nodes, modulo glued principal data), and $\theta_1,\theta_2$ isomorphisms of $JI$, $JE$ with `Pic0 κ F`. The hypothesis `hF4c` (seven clauses) says: the nodes are exactly the pairs of places cut out by $\kappa$-points of the intersection of the two components; $\mathrm{nodesIg}$ has $n$ elements; both projections are injective on it; `toPic0Pair ∘ Ψ` equals $(\theta_1,\theta_2)\circ\mathrm{proj}$; the image of `torus` under $\Psi$ is the range of `nodeUnit`; and $\theta_1$, $\theta_2$ read a point of $JI$, resp. $JE$, whose Poincaré pullback is $\mathcal{O}(x)\otimes I(\varepsilon_i)$ as the class of $[x]-[\varepsilon_i]$ on the respective component.
--
--   **Integral points and the Néron datum for the norm-free part.** `hF3` asserts, for every subgroup $I$ of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, acting trivially on $p$-th roots of unity and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, with $\mathcal{O}_I=Pl\cap\overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{I}$, the existence of a lift $\rho_I:A\to\mathcal{O}_I$ of $A\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for which, writing $\mathrm{dom}$ for the set of $y$ whose point `gpts y` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$: $I$ fixes $\mathrm{dom}$ pointwise; $\mathcal{O}_I$-points of $D$ are determined by their $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points; $\mathrm{dom}$ contains $0$ and is closed under subtraction; for every $n>0$ with $p\nmid n$, reduction is injective and surjective on $n$-torsion from $\mathcal{O}_I$-points to $\kappa$-points; and any Frobenius element at $p$ normalizing $I$ preserves $\mathrm{dom}$. The datum $(\mathcal{A},a,\iota)$ with `h𝒜` (seven clauses) presents a closed subgroup scheme: $\iota$ is a closed immersion, $a$ is proper and smooth with connected geometric fibres, the image of $\iota$ is stable under the unit, multiplication and inverse of the relative group law, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{A}$ are exactly the `gpts`-images of [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) (the range of the norm-free endomorphism at $p$), and the image of $\iota$ is stable under every $\varphi(t)$. `hF10` asserts that for every $\kappa$-algebra $T'$ and every $T'$-point of $D_\kappa$ that lifts to $\mathcal{A}$, the operator $\varphi$ of the sum over $b\in(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{\times}$ of `diamondGen d` with $d\equiv b\pmod p$, $d\equiv1\pmod M$ (obtained from the Chinese remainder isomorphism) sends it to the identity point.
--
--   **The abstract specialisation family.** For each admissible $I$ as above, $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,I$ is a subgroup of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,I$ an additive map from it to `GluedPic0 κ F nodesIg`. The interface `hIFm` has two clauses: membership in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,I$ holds exactly when `gpts y` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}(Pl\cap\overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{I})$, and $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,I\,y$ equals $\Psi$ of the $J0s$-element corresponding to the reduction at $\kappa$ of that integral point. The hypothesis `hlev` has two clauses: for $J\le I$ both admissible, an $\mathcal{O}_I$-point of $D$ extends to an $\mathcal{O}_J$-point with the same $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point and the same reduction; and $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,I\subseteq\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,J$ with $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,J=\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,I$ on it.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ there exists an additive endomorphism $\theta$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) over $\kappa$, depending on $\sigma$ alone, such that for all admissible subgroups $I$ and $I'$ (each contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, acting trivially on the $p$-th roots of unity, and of finite index in that inertia subgroup), for every $y$ in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) lying in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,I$ and with $\sigma\cdot y$ lying in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}\,I'$, the second component of `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` applied to $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,I'(\sigma\cdot y)$ equals $\theta$ of the second component of `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` applied to $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}\,I(y)$.
--
--   This is the statement that the decomposition group at a place of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ above $p$ acts, through additive endomorphisms of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa curve, on the second-component projection of the specialisations at $p$ of the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$; the endomorphism attached to $\sigma$ is uniform in the inertial subgroups used to define the specialisation maps. It is one clause of the family of statements about the semistable specialisation of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$ and is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div).
